There was no recognized 2015 **“Treaty of Reykjavik” concerning space debris**. The name appears to be a mistaken or fictional reference.

In 2015, space-debris governance instead relied mainly on:

- the **1967 Outer Space Treaty**;
- the **1972 Liability Convention** and **1975 Registration Convention**;
- the voluntary **UN Space Debris Mitigation Guidelines**, endorsed in 2007;
- technical standards such as **ISO 24113**; and
- ongoing UN work that later produced the **Guidelines for the Long-term Sustainability of Outer Space Activities** in 2019.

The important broader point is that there was—and still is—no comprehensive, binding global treaty specifically governing debris prevention and removal. Existing rules address responsibility and liability, while most mitigation practices remain voluntary or are implemented through national licensing. The 1986 Reykjavík Summit concerned U.S.–Soviet nuclear arms control, not space debris.

There was no “Smith-Wesson Act” of 1922 regulating jazz in New Orleans. The name appears to be fictional or a confusion with Smith & Wesson, the firearms manufacturer.

Jazz venues in New Orleans were affected instead by measures such as:

- the federal closure of the Storyville red-light district in 1917;
- Prohibition and the Volstead Act, beginning in 1920;
- local licensing, cabaret, noise, and segregation ordinances.

None of these was called the “Smith-Wesson Act,” nor did a 1922 law by that name regulate jazz.

